<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Ashkelon</h1><p class="st-meta">1175 BCE – 604 BCE (approx.) · capital Ashkelon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Ashkelon, the Philistine seaport of the southern Levant, whose Iron Age cemetery has revealed the Aegean origins of its settlers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ashkelon had been a large Canaanite harbour town since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a>, and its kings wrote to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>. In the 12th century BCE <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/philistine-pentapolis">Philistines</a> settled there, and it became one of the cities of the Philistine Pentapolis.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its kings ruled a busy port trading wine and oil, with a market in the city centre. Excavations led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lawrence-stager">Lawrence Stager</a> uncovered a Philistine cemetery, and a 2019 DNA study found that its early inhabitants included newcomers of southern European ancestry. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidqa">Sidqa</a> rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a> in 701 BCE and was deported.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ashkelon remained an Assyrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a> destroyed it in 604 BCE and deported its king Aga to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. The city later revived as a Phoenician and Greek port.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ashkelon.jpg" alt="Ashkelon" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: דוידי ורדי · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:93439_marina_ashkelon_PikiWiki_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1175 BCE – 604 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ashkelon</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sidqa, Mitinti, Aga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Philistine religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Philistine, Canaanite</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Philistine Ashkelon</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-890&amp;focus=ashkelon" aria-label="Ashkelon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5571 1025 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ashkelon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5891 1281L6092 1134M5891 1281L6140 1251"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6092 1134h0M6140 1251h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5891 1281h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5891 1281h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-890&amp;focus=ashkelon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

